---
/**
 * ProofGate — section 2 of the homepage: the proof point, and the gate.
 *
 * WHERE IT COMES FROM. Owner instruction, 2026-08-04: the two cards that open
 * concepts/homepage-v2.html become the homepage's SECOND section rather than
 * its hero. In the concept they carry the h1, the lede and the buttons; here
 * the hero above already carries all three, so what moves across is the
 * argument the two cards make and nothing else. The buttons did not move: a
 * second "Book a demo" pair 400px under the hero's own pair is two offers of
 * the same thing, and FinalCta closes the page with the third.
 *
 * ── "UK PUBLIC PROCUREMENT" IS THE PROOF POINT, NOT THE MARKET ──────────────
 *
 * This is the one thing about this section that must not be got wrong, and it
 * has been got wrong before. OA-25, recorded in HeroStack.astro's header: "the
 * site's narrative is now market-neutral with UK public kept as the proof point
 * rather than as the whole story." OA-33 draws the line the wording has to
 * respect: UK may describe the SUBJECT and may never describe the PRODUCT.
 *
 * So the eyebrow names the market this product is proved in, the left card
 * describes what it does there, and the last sentence of that card says in as
 * many words that the same engine runs a private-sector bid. The hero says
 * nothing about sector at all, which is why this section can afford to be
 * specific. Deleting that closing sentence would turn the section back into the
 * claim OA-25 exists to stop.
 *
 * ── WHY THE REFUSED EXAMPLE IS THE SAME ONE THE TRACE USES ──────────────────
 *
 * "Local supply chain spend, no recorded commitment" is the exact figure
 * ReasoningTrace drops at stage 02, five sections below. That is deliberate: a
 * reader meets the refusal as a claim here and watches the same claim being
 * refused there, so the page argues once rather than twice. If either one is
 * reworded, reword both.
 *
 * ── NOTHING HERE DECLARES A RECIPE ─────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The cards are `.surface .surface--pad`, the row is `.card-row`, the labels
 * are `.eyebrow` and `.meta`, and the refused slab is a `.surface--flat` inside
 * a card. Every one of those is defined once, elsewhere. What this file writes
 * is spacing, and one border colour that carries meaning.
 */
import Section from '../layout/Section.astro';
---

{/*
  `sv-floor`, on the <section> so it runs the full viewport width. Sections
  alternate floor and base down the page so a raised panel always has something
  to be raised above (styles/surfaces.css). The hero above is base, this is
  floor, the product showcase below is base again.
*/}
{/*
  NO HEAD BLOCK, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE SECTION. Section.astro renders an
  eyebrow, a heading and a standfirst when it is given them, and it is given
  none: the two cards carry their own labels and their own headings, which is
  what the concept draws and what makes them read as two statements rather than
  as one section's two examples. Section is still the shell, so the measure, the
  gutter and the vertical padding all come from the same place as every other
  section on the page.
*/}
{/*
  ── EIGHT AND FOUR, NOT FIFTY-FIFTY ─────────────────────────────────────────

  Owner, 2026-08-04, on the built page: "we must keep cards sizes exactly same
  as http://127.0.0.1:8712/concepts/homepage-v2.html". These two cards shipped
  as `.card-row--pair`, which is two equal tracks — measured at 1440, 512px
  beside 512px. The concept draws them as `col-8` and `col-4` of a twelve-column
  grid: 814.67px beside 397.34px, a ratio of 2.05. Two equal cards say "here are
  two things"; eight and four say "here is the argument, and here is the gate on
  it", which is what this section is.

  The modifier is `.card-row--eight-four` in styles/card-row.css, added in the
  same pass and argued there. It reproduces the concept's twelve-track geometry
  AND its 20px gutter, the second of which was left at the site's value for one
  build and was wrong to leave: on a twelve-track grid the gutter is inside both
  cards as well as between them, seven times in the wide one and three in the
  narrow, so it sets the SPLIT and not only the space. At the site's 40px the
  two came out 696/328 against the concept's 814.67/397.34.
*/}
<Section id="proof" class="sv-floor">
  <div class="card-row card-row--eight-four pg">
    <article class="surface surface--panel surface--pad pg__card">
      <p class="eyebrow">UK public procurement</p>

      <h2 class="pg__h">The engine that refuses to make things up.</h2>

      <p class="pg__p">
        The hardest market to bluff in is the one with a published rulebook, so it is the one the
        engine is proved against. CrowAgent reads both UK registers, drafts answers cited to the
        published requirement, to your own past bids and to commitments you have confirmed, and
        evidences delivery after award.
      </p>

      <p class="pg__p">
        The same engine runs a private-sector bid. What changes is the rulebook it cites, not the
        refusal.
      </p>

      <p class="pg__p pg__note">
        Access is by request. There is no free plan and no self-serve trial.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="surface surface--panel surface--pad pg__card pg__card--gate">
      {/*
        `eyebrow--marker` turns the spectrum gradient off so the call site's own
        colour comes through, which is exactly what labels.css wrote it for: an
        eyebrow that is also a marker. The hue is the refusal role and nothing
        else on this page may borrow it.
      */}
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--marker pg__gate">Refusal gate</p>

      <h2 class="pg__h">A figure with nothing behind it does not survive the draft.</h2>

      <p class="pg__p">
        Every pound and every percentage in a CrowAgent answer is computed from a commitment you
        have confirmed. If the commitment is not there, the claim is taken out before a named human
        reviews the answer.
      </p>

      {/*
        The slab is a card inside a card, which is what `surface--flat` is for:
        the same glass with no cast shadow, so it reads as a panel set into the
        card rather than as a second card floating on top of it. Only the border
        colour is stated here, and it is stated because it means something.
      */}
      <div class="surface surface--flat pg__slab">
        <p class="meta pg__slab-key">Refused</p>
        <p class="pg__p">
          Local supply chain spend. No recorded commitment sits behind the figure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  /* `surface--panel` ON BOTH CARDS, ADDED 2026-08-05. The concept's `.card` is
     a SOLID `background-color: var(--c-panel)` — rgb(12, 16, 32) — and the
     default `.surface` here is the 5% white glass, which measured as
     rgba(255,255,255,0.05) over whatever the section behind it happened to be.
     `surface--panel` is the site's own opaque card variant and it already holds
     --c-panel at exactly the concept's value, so this is a variant swap rather
     than a new recipe and nothing about the border, radius, padding or hover
     changes. The specular inset and the cast shadow the concept does not have
     are still there; they belong to `.surface` sitewide and removing them is a
     separate decision. */

  /* The two cards lay their own children out. `.card-row > .surface` already
     makes them columns; this is only the rhythm inside one. */
  .pg__card > * {
    margin-block-end: var(--stack-2);
  }

  /* ── THE VOID AT THE FOOT OF THE WIDE CARD, WHICH IS WHAT "LOOKS EMPTY" WAS ─
   *
   * Owner, 2026-08-05 (A-104 item 3). Rendered and looked at rather than
   * inferred from measurements, which is the only reason this was found: the
   * numbers said the two cards were the same height and both close to the
   * concept, and they were. The screenshot said the wide card carried roughly
   * 200px of dead space along its bottom edge.
   *
   * MECHANISM. The row stretches both cards to the taller one, and the gate
   * card is taller because it ends in a slab. `.card-row > .surface` already
   * makes each card a column flex container, and the comment on that rule says
   * it exists "so that a short card distributes its own slack rather than
   * leaving a hole" — but a column flex container defaults to
   * `justify-content: flex-start`, so the slack was never distributed at all.
   * The content pinned to the top and the hole formed underneath it.
   *
   * THE CONCEPT ANTICIPATED THIS AND SOLVED IT THE SAME WAY. Its equivalent of
   * this card is `.hero-main { display: flex; flex-direction: column;
   * justify-content: center }` (homepage-v2.html line 176). Copying that value
   * is both the fidelity fix and the right fix.
   *
   * ONLY THE WIDE CARD. The gate card must NOT be centred: it ends with the
   * refusal slab, which the concept pushes to the foot with `margin-top: auto`,
   * and centring the column would pull that slab up off the bottom edge and
   * break the pairing the two cards make. */
  .pg__card:not(.pg__card--gate) {
    justify-content: center;
  }
  .pg__card > :last-child {
    margin-block-end: 0;
  }

  /* ── THE CAPSULE HAS TO SAY IT IS NOT A BANNER ──────────────────────────────
   *
   * `.card-row > .surface` makes every card a COLUMN FLEX CONTAINER so that a
   * short card distributes its own slack rather than leaving a hole. The cost is
   * that a flex item is blockified and then stretched on the cross axis, which
   * on a column is the horizontal one: `.eyebrow` is `display: inline-block` in
   * labels.css and would arrive here as a full-width pill running the whole card.
   * That is the same trap the concept's own hero card hit and left a note about.
   *
   * `center` rather than `start`, because the head block of every section on this
   * site is centred and these two cards ARE the section's head block. The capsule
   * keeps its own width and sits on the card's axis with the heading under it. */
  .pg__card > .eyebrow {
    align-self: center;
  }

  /* ── THE GATE CARD IS LIT DIFFERENTLY FROM THE ARGUMENT CARD ──────────────
   *
   * The concept's narrow card carries
   * `linear-gradient(150deg, var(--c-panel), color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-panel)
   * 55%, var(--c-floor)))` — the panel colour falling away towards the page
   * floor across a 150 degree diagonal. Reproduced here character for character
   * from the same three tokens, which this site already holds at the concept's
   * exact values: --c-panel is #0C1020 in both, --c-floor is #03040A in both.
   *
   * It is written at the point of use rather than as a --grad-* token because
   * check-design-system rule 7 was amended on 2026-08-03 to assert what it
   * actually cares about — no literal colour in a gradient — after the token
   * form was found to bake in its own var() fallbacks and paint the wrong
   * pixels. Every colour here resolves from a var().
   *
   * `background-image` ALONE, never the `background` shorthand: the shorthand
   * would also reset `.surface`'s background-color, and this gradient is opaque
   * at both ends so the card would look right and the recipe would be forked.
   * The border, the radius, the shadow and the hover all stay `.surface`'s.
   */
  .pg__card--gate {
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      150deg,
      var(--c-panel),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-panel) 55%, var(--c-floor))
    );
  }

  /* --t-h3 on an h2, which check-design-system permits at one tier down and
     which is the documented convention for a heading inside a card. Two of
     these sit side by side with no section title above them, so they are the
     section's headings rather than a section heading's subordinates, and they
     take the size a card heading takes. */
  .pg__h {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h3);
    line-height: var(--lh-heading);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--c-text);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* Ink and rhythm only. The size comes from the base `p` rule in tokens.css
     and the leading from --lh-body, so this states nothing the site has not
     already decided. */
  .pg__p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
    /* ── A MEASURE, WHICH IS HALF OF WHY THIS CARD "LOOKED EMPTY" ────────────
     *
     * Owner, 2026-08-05 (A-104 item 3): the UK public procurement card looks
     * empty. Two causes, and this is the one that is not about size at all.
     * The wide card is 814.67px across, so with no cap these paragraphs ran the
     * full ~747px of its content box: roughly 90 characters a line at --t-body,
     * well past a comfortable measure, which reads as thin text stretched
     * across a wide box rather than as a column of prose. The concept does not
     * do this. Its equivalent paragraph carries `.measure` (homepage-v2.html
     * line 91, `max-width: 62ch`) inside a card of the same width.
     *
     * 39.113em IS THAT 62ch, AND THE NUMBER IS BORROWED RATHER THAN INVENTED.
     * styles/headings.css:340 derives it by measurement: the rendered cap was
     * 813.55px at --t-lede's 20.8px, and 813.55 / 20.8 = 39.113. Expressed in
     * em it holds the same rendered width at every size while losing the
     * dependence on which font happens to have loaded, which is the whole point
     * of A-92's ch-to-em change. Writing `62ch` here would have reintroduced
     * the exact CLS mechanism that work removed. */
    max-width: 39.113em;
    /* THE CAP HAS TO STAY ON THE CARD'S AXIS. These cards centre their content,
       so a capped block left at `margin-inline: 0` would sit hard against the
       inline start edge while the heading and the capsule above it stayed
       centred, which reads as a broken column rather than as a measure. Written
       as `margin-inline` and not the `margin` shorthand on purpose: the
       shorthand would also reset the block margins that `.pg__card > *` uses
       for the rhythm between children. */
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /* ── AND THE OTHER HALF: THE WIDE CARD HAD NO FOCAL POINT ────────────────
   *
   * Both cards' headings sat at --t-h3, so the 8-column card and the 4-column
   * card had the same largest element and the wide one had nothing to anchor
   * it. The concept does not split them that way: its equivalent of THIS card
   * carries the page's biggest text (`.t-hero`, up to 73.6px) while its narrow
   * gate card is at `.t-card`, 20.8px. That contrast is what stops the wide one
   * reading as under-filled.
   *
   * --t-h2 rather than --t-h1, deliberately. This site has a separate hero
   * section above, which the concept does not, and two display-scale headlines
   * stacked would compete. --t-h2 is the section tier, which is what this card
   * actually is: the opening statement of section 2. The gate card stays at
   * --t-h3, so the hierarchy between the two is now stated rather than absent. */
  .pg__card:not(.pg__card--gate) .pg__h {
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
  }

  .pg__note {
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* The refusal hue, on the label and on the slab's edge. Both are markers: the
     word "Refused" and the panel it heads are the one thing on this card the
     colour is carrying, and removing all colour leaves the word doing the same
     job on its own, which is WCAG 1.4.1. `border-color` alone, never a `border`
     shorthand, so the width and style stay `.surface`'s to state. */
  /* THE TINT AND THE EDGE ARE THE CONCEPT'S, to the percentage: 14% fill and a
     42% edge of the refusal hue, which is what makes this read as a stamp
     rather than as another section label. `background-color` and `border-color`
     only — the radius, the padding and the type are still `.eyebrow`'s in
     labels.css, so this does not become a sixth declaration of the capsule and
     check-treatments rule 9 has nothing to fire on. */
  .pg__gate {
    color: var(--c-refused);
    background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 14%, transparent);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 42%, transparent);
  }

  .pg__slab {
    /* --blk-pad-2xs, the flat 16px tier, because this slab sits inside a card
       that is only a third of the row.

       THE FIFTH TIER NOW EXISTS AND THIS IS WHAT IT IS FOR. This block used to
       say a fifth tier for one slab "would be a number wearing a name" and sat
       6px wide of the concept on purpose. That reasoning was wrong in one
       specific way: 16px is not an extra number invented for this slab, it is
       the bottom rung of the concept's own five-step ladder (40/36/28/22/16),
       and the ladder here simply stopped at four. tokens.css now carries all
       five. Measured 22 against the concept's 16 at every viewport before the
       change.
       Padding only; the radius stays `.surface`'s. */
    padding: var(--blk-pad-2xs);
    /* 10px, against the 16px this used to inherit from `.surface`. The concept
       puts the slab on --r-sm, its smallest radius, and the harness read
       radius 10 vs 16 at every viewport.

       THIS DOES NOT TRIP check-design-system RULE 3, and by construction rather
       than by exemption. Rule 3 fires on radius + padding + paint TOGETHER, and
       only when the radius is at card scale; its own comment names --radius-chip
       at 10px as what separates a chip from a card, which is why .rt__drop-box
       and .in__chip sit outside the test. A slab quoted inside a card is below
       card scale in exactly that sense, and the concept agrees by giving it the
       small radius rather than the card one. */
    border-radius: var(--radius-chip);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 45%, transparent);
    /* The concept sets the slab on --c-floor, the page's deepest step, so it
       reads as a panel cut INTO the card rather than laid on top of it. Same
       token, same value (#03040A on both). `.surface--flat` gives it the glass
       with no cast shadow; this gives it the floor. */
    background-color: var(--c-floor);
  }

  .pg__slab-key {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    color: var(--c-refused);
  }

  /* The slab sits at the foot of its card whatever the copy above it does, so
     the two cards' last elements line up on a row that shares a floor. */
  .pg__card > .pg__slab {
    margin-block-start: auto;
  }
</style>
